{
 "schema": 1,
 "_doc": "THE PRINTED LENGTH OF THE CHICAGO POST OFFICE'S 1 APRIL 1834 RETURN, COUNTED OFF THE PAGE IMAGE (T-1141, in T-0424's shape for the 1 January return). Every line of the list as it stands in its SECOND printing -- Chicago Democrat Vol. I No. 19 of 1834-04-08, page 3, printed column 3 -- read at the scan and set down in printed order, left sub-column first. T-1138 read three lines of this return at three impressions and could not avoid seeing what stood around them; this is the rest of the page. `as_printed` is this impression's own setting, verbatim and unamended -- `Josept Babeax`, `Hexekiah Duncklo`, `Jomes Mulford`, `Dadly Peck` stand as the type stands, and `Geo. Squar` carries no `e` because the type sets none. A `[?]` inside a name is a glyph the image does not carry legibly and `[x]` is a glyph read under doubt; a trailing digit is the count of letters waiting, which is how this list marks them. NOTHING HERE IS A RULING ON A CARD: this file is the READING, the concordance beside it is what each line reaches, and moving a card is a third decision that belongs to T-1153.",
 "return": {
  "office": "Chicago, Illinois",
  "remaining_as_of": "1834-04-01",
  "printed_heading": "REMAINING in the Post Office at Chicago, Ill. on the first day of April, 1834.",
  "signed": "JOHN S. C. HOGAN, P. M.",
  "footer": "*.*Persons calling for any of the above letters will please say they are advertised.",
  "sub_columns": 2
 },
 "printing_read": {
  "issue_id": "chicago_democrat_1834_04_08",
  "publication": "chicago_democrat",
  "volume": "I",
  "number": "19",
  "date": "1834-04-08",
  "issue_page": 3,
  "printed_column": 3,
  "which_printing": "second of three (1834-04-01 No. 18, this one, and 1834-04-16 No. 20)",
  "why_this_one": "T-1138 read all three impressions at these pages and recorded which is usable: the 1 April setting has the next column's Public Auction advertisement bleeding through the S run, and the 16 April setting is a fresh, narrower setting that loses the tail of most surnames -- its own extraction brackets them for that reason. The 8 April impression is the one whose column stands clear of both faults for its whole length, which is what a line-by-line count of the WHOLE return needs. It is also a different impression from the one claim c002 of 1834-04-01 was transcribed from, and that is stated rather than hidden: a line read here is this setting's line, and where the two settings could disagree the concordance carries both readings instead of choosing."
 },
